{"h":3209,"lines":[{"y":0.040477410009711168,"h":0.013521924122843032,"x":0.089235873795228529,"w":0.060363203684488928,"t":"Test 4","c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.088662790786161572,"w":0.30873786926269531,"x":0.089320385717966716,"h":0.016010106166946536,"t":"READING PASSAGE 2"},{"h":0.020518462754484323,"y":0.12606621116426198,"c":1,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14-26, which are based on Reading","w":0.82535105387369789,"x":0.08927374010848553},{"c":1,"t":"Passage 2 below.","w":0.16893203735351564,"h":0.015988371899566145,"y":0.14825581403879662,"x":0.089320388972340695},{"x":0.35728155242186349,"c":1,"w":0.30291262308756511,"h":0.026310403176185826,"y":0.18459302307993097,"t":"The growth mindset"},{"h":0.020751363688911617,"y":0.22695187766324953,"t":"Over the past century, a powerful idea has taken root in the educational landscape. The concept of","w":0.83704956054687507,"c":1,"x":0.089252968433693625},{"w":0.83709904988606765,"y":0.245016736293451,"c":1,"x":0.087287444062717254,"h":0.02163758797808979,"t":"intelligence as something innate has been supplanted by the idea that intelligence is not fixed, and"},{"c":1,"y":0.26444326393650486,"w":0.83511220296223954,"t":"that, with the right training, we can be the authors of our own cognitive capabilities. Psychologist","x":0.087303192735473378,"h":0.020595543406834116},{"t":"Alfred Binet, the developer of the first intelligence tests, was one of many 19th-century scientists","y":0.28161509507378624,"x":0.089187626474254295,"h":0.023436142870941645,"w":0.8293862915039063,"c":1},{"y":0.30087209311066965,"w":0.8349514261881511,"h":0.0188953482844747,"c":1,"x":0.089320407449613795,"t":"who held that earlier view and sought to quantify cognitive ability. Then, in the early 20th century,"},{"t":"progressive thinkers revolted against the notion that inherent ability is destiny. Instead, educators","h":0.020944667828045826,"c":1,"x":0.087318333365912229,"w":0.82923980712890633,"y":0.31925614013366266},{"h":0.021556179248655272,"c":1,"t":"such as John Dewey argued that every child's intelligence could be developed, given the right","x":0.089232703141047032,"y":0.33850385079295464,"w":0.80599685668945309},{"c":1,"h":0.016731029284706045,"x":0.088933649288543346,"y":0.35968214249642738,"t":"environment.","w":0.11736053466796874},{"y":0.39532710282922179,"t":"'Growth mindset theory' is a relatively new - and extremely popular - version of this idea. In","h":0.0174635949535904,"c":1,"x":0.087378640678413055,"w":0.80000000000000004},{"w":0.79439773559570315,"c":1,"x":0.089190879469541448,"y":0.41090110865696505,"h":0.023213999145127562,"t":"many schools today you will see hallways covered in motivational posters and hear speeches"},{"w":0.81747573852539057,"c":1,"y":0.43302180679323887,"h":0.017559588661074987,"t":"on the mindset of great sporting heroes who simply believed their way to the top. A major focus","x":0.089320383185374219},{"y":0.4502657997480175,"x":0.089238036608854737,"t":"of the growth mindset in schools is coaxing students away from seeing failure as an indication","c":1,"w":0.80598937988281261,"h":0.020887952430226009},{"w":0.81359222412109378,"c":1,"t":"of their ability, and towards seeing it as a chance to improve that ability. As educationalist Jeff","y":0.46947040505108673,"h":0.019003115264797588,"x":0.089320392701726328},{"w":0.82524271647135428,"x":0.089320389287917412,"t":"Howard noted several decades ago: 'Smart is not something that you just are, smart is something","c":1,"h":0.018895348284474811,"y":0.48837209329522868},{"t":"that you can get.'","h":0.018726821851878905,"c":1,"y":0.50848020399050942,"w":0.15204350789388021,"x":0.089122605838787811},{"y":0.54313775074029369,"t":"The idea of the growth mindset is based on the work of psychologist Carol Dweck in California","c":1,"x":0.089234131982488238,"w":0.81765960693359385,"h":0.021375193848416796},{"w":0.78640777587890631,"y":0.56250000008741374,"x":0.087378633633362499,"h":0.018895348284474811,"t":"in the 1990s. In one key experiment, Dweck divided a group of 10- to 12-year-olds into two","c":1},{"h":0.020853698587863234,"w":0.83320454915364572,"c":1,"y":0.58093136562850933,"t":"groups. All were told that they had achieved a high score on a test but the first group were praised","x":0.089226194966872091},{"t":"for their intelligence in achieving this, while the others were praised for their effort. The second","w":0.81764027913411463,"h":0.02127734983450158,"y":0.59948323925578617,"c":1,"x":0.089244003692401278},{"x":0.089265474546179086,"w":0.83698689778645841,"h":0.021042972933094872,"c":1,"t":"group - those who had been instilled with a 'growth mindset' - were subsequently far more likely","y":0.61713602640056131},{"w":0.79428349812825516,"h":0.020190959704627809,"t":"to put effort into future tasks. Meanwhile, the former took on only those tasks that would not","c":1,"x":0.089270881102043426,"y":0.63798590480283424},{"h":0.021182816645066715,"y":0.65526319858474313,"c":1,"x":0.089265112740262678,"w":0.8136818440755208,"t":"risk their sense of worth. This group had inferred that success or failure is due to innate ability,"},{"t":"and this 'fixed mindset' had led them to fear of failure and lack of effort. Praising ability actually","w":0.82718444824218751,"h":0.017585851247436945,"x":0.089320397170168347,"c":1,"y":0.67587209290998362},{"t":"made the students perform worse, while praising effort emphasised that change was possible.","w":0.7982393391927084,"h":0.023378476769753354,"x":0.089222288724901469,"y":0.69297755825993834,"c":1},{"w":0.77669901529947916,"t":"One of the greatest impediments to successfully implementing a growth mindset, however,","y":0.73255813941929582,"x":0.091262142825595638,"h":0.01744186068621012,"c":1},{"t":"is the education system itself: in many parts of the world, the school climate is obsessed","w":0.75145629882812504,"h":0.018895348284474811,"x":0.089320394178421997,"y":0.75000000027391966,"c":1},{"t":"with performance in the form of constant testing, analysing and ranking of students - a key","x":0.091206797433823011,"h":0.020247528931804926,"y":0.76853663859938603,"w":0.77874796549479175,"c":1},{"h":0.015988371899566145,"c":1,"x":0.091262149672153334,"w":0.78446599324544275,"t":"characteristic of the fixed mindset. Nor is it unusual for schools to create a certain cognitive","y":0.78779069775255606},{"y":0.80454950638406386,"x":0.09116105563732238,"t":"dissonance, when they applaud the benefits of a growth mindset but then hand out fixed target","w":0.80407277425130208,"h":0.021470677741220023,"c":1},{"x":0.091262138655587813,"h":0.017478084861303866,"w":0.34757280985514327,"c":1,"t":"grades in lessons based on performance.","y":0.8255451710550461},{"c":1,"x":0.091196309406764153,"t":"Aside from the implementation problem, the original growth mindset research has also received","y":0.861128816527026,"w":0.81762303670247394,"h":0.020683305211527725},{"t":"harsh criticism. The statistician Andrew Gelman claims that 'their research designs have","x":0.091262124477657955,"y":0.88068535825901018,"w":0.7533980814615886,"h":0.017570455794765238,"c":1},{"w":0.76710993448893228,"t":"enough degrees of freedom that they could take their data to support just about any theory","x":0.091204270235397372,"y":0.89891271885613877,"h":0.019929474090861321,"c":1},{"t":"88","x":0.091262136018138013,"c":1,"w":0.025242718060811367,"h":0.01308139551465759,"y":0.95784883707607915}],"w":2400}